@startuml Load_Balancer_Architecture

title Load Balancer — Quality Attributes + 4 Types

skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam roundcorner 10
skinparam shadowing false

skinparam rectangle {
    BorderColor #333333
    FontColor #222222
}
skinparam package {
    BorderColor #555555
    FontColor #222222
}

rectangle "Load Balancer\n(Distributes traffic + abstracts\nserver group as one unit)" as LB #F4F6F7

package "Quality Attributes Provided" as QA #FDFEFE {
    rectangle "Scalability\n(add/remove servers,\nauto-scaling)" as Q1 #AED6F1
    rectangle "Availability\n(skip unhealthy servers)" as Q2 #AED6F1
    rectangle "Performance\n(higher throughput,\nslight latency cost)" as Q3 #AED6F1
    rectangle "Maintainability\n(rolling releases,\nno disruption)" as Q4 #AED6F1
}

LB -down-> QA

package "4 Types of Load Balancing" as Types #F4F6F7 {

    rectangle "1. DNS Load Balancing\n\nMaps domain -> IP list\nRound-robin rotation" as T1 #F5B7B1

    rectangle "2. Hardware LB\n\nDedicated physical device" as T2 #A9DFBF

    rectangle "3. Software LB\n\nProgram on general\npurpose computer" as T3 #A9DFBF

    rectangle "4. GSLB\n(Global Server LB)\n\nDNS + intelligent routing\nacross data centers" as T4 #F9E79F
}

QA -down-> Types

note bottom of T1
  Drawbacks: no health check,
  simple round-robin only,
  exposes server IPs (less secure)
end note

note bottom of T2
  Same benefits as Software LB:
  - Hides server IPs (secure)
  - Active health checks
  - Smart, weighted routing
  - Must be collocated with servers
end note

note bottom of T4
  Routes by: location, current
  data center load, or best
  response time. Enables disaster
  recovery across regions.
end note

@enduml
